<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Install Dual</title>
<meta name="description" content="Dual: SMS and free online chat in one app. Download and install steps.">
<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #f5f8ff; --card: #ffffff; --text: #101828; --muted: #5b6577; --line: #dde4f0;
    --a: #19a7ff; --b: #3d5afe; --c: #14baff; --code: #eef2fb;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root { --bg: #0b0f1a; --card: #131a2a; --text: #eef2fb; --muted: #9aa6bd; --line: #243049; --code: #0b0f1a; }
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body { margin: 0; background: var(--bg); color: var(--text);
         font: 16px/1.55 system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif; }
  main { max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 28px 18px 60px; }
  .logo { width: 96px; height: 96px; border-radius: 26px; display: block; margin: 8px auto 14px;
          background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--a), var(--b) 50%, var(--c)); position: relative; }
  .logo svg { position: absolute; inset: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; }
  h1 { text-align: center; margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: 30px; }
  .tag { text-align: center; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 22px; }
  .btn { display: block; text-align: center; text-decoration: none; color: #fff; font-weight: 700;
         font-size: 18px; padding: 16px; border-radius: 999px;
         background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--b), var(--a)); }
  .meta { text-align: center; color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; margin: 10px 0 26px; }
  .card { background: var(--card); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 18px;
          padding: 18px 20px; margin: 16px 0; }
  h2 { font-size: 18px; margin: 0 0 10px; }
  ol, ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; }
  li { margin: 7px 0; }
  .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: 14px; }
  code { background: var(--code); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 8px; padding: 2px 6px;
         font-size: 12.5px; word-break: break-all; }
  details { margin: 8px 0; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; }
  footer { text-align: center; color: var(--muted); font-size: 13px; margin-top: 30px; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
  <div class="logo" aria-hidden="true">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 108 108">
      <g transform="translate(54 54) scale(1.18) translate(-54 -54)">
        <path fill="#fff" fill-opacity="0.62" d="M34,28 H58 A6,6 0 0 1 64,34 V50 A6,6 0 0 1 58,56 H34 A6,6 0 0 1 28,50 V34 A6,6 0 0 1 34,28 Z M32,54 L32,66 L46,54 Z"/>
        <path fill="#fff" d="M50,42 H74 A6,6 0 0 1 80,48 V64 A6,6 0 0 1 74,70 H50 A6,6 0 0 1 44,64 V48 A6,6 0 0 1 50,42 Z M78,68 L78,80 L64,68 Z"/>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <h1>Dual</h1>
  <p class="tag">SMS and free online chat, in one app.</p>

  <a class="btn" href="{{DOWNLOAD_URL}}">Download Dual for Android</a>
  <p class="meta">Version <span id="v-name">{{VERSION_NAME}}</span> &middot; <span id="v-size">{{APK_SIZE_MB}}</span> MB &middot; updated <span id="v-date">{{DATE}}</span><br>No account needed to download.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>How to install</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Download</b> the file with the button above. Your browser may say "this type of file can harm your device". That is a standard message for any app installed outside the Play Store. Choose <b>Download anyway</b> or <b>OK</b>.</li>
      <li><b>Open</b> the downloaded file. If Android asks, tap <b>Settings</b> and allow installs from this browser, then go back.</li>
      <li>If <b>Play Protect</b> shows a warning, tap <b>More details</b>, then <b>Install anyway</b>. (It appears because Dual is new and not on the Play Store yet.)</li>
      <li>Open <b>Dual</b>, tap <b>Get started</b> and allow the permissions it asks for. They are for reading and sending text messages, showing your contacts, and notifications.</li>
      <li>Sign up with your email. A code is sent to it to confirm it is you.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Android says a setting is "restricted"?</h2>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:0">Apps installed from a file are blocked from becoming the default text-message app until you allow it. Do this once:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Try to turn on <b>Dual as your SMS app</b> in the app (so Android registers the block).</li>
      <li>Open <b>Settings &rarr; Apps &rarr; Dual</b>, tap the <b>&#8942;</b> menu (top right).</li>
      <li>Choose <b>Allow restricted settings</b> and confirm.</li>
      <li>Go back to Dual and turn the switch on again.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Is it safe?</h2>
    <p style="margin-top:0">Dual is made by BinaryWaves. It is not on the Play Store yet, so Android shows warnings it shows for every app installed from a file. Dual keeps your text messages on your phone. To check that the file you downloaded is the real one:</p>
    <details>
      <summary>Show the file fingerprints</summary>
      <p class="note">Download file (SHA-256):<br><code id="v-sha">{{APK_SHA256}}</code></p>
      <p class="note">Signing certificate (SHA-256):<br><code>{{CERT_SHA256}}</code></p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a GitHub account?</summary>
      <p class="note">No. The download button works for anyone, with no account and no login.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Why isn't it on the Play Store?</summary>
      <p class="note">Dual is new and independent, and publishing there has a fee. It may be added later. Until then, new versions are announced inside the app and installed the same way.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How do I update?</summary>
      <p class="note">When a new version is out, a banner appears at the top of your chats. Tap it, download, and install over the old one. Your messages stay.</p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <footer>Dual &middot; Developed by BinaryWaves</footer>
</main>
<script>
// Keeps the version, size, date and fingerprint current from latest.json, so the page
// is right the moment a release is published, without uploading this page again. If
// the browser cannot read it, the values written when the page was made stay.
(function () {
  function set(id, value) { var e = document.getElementById(id); if (e && value) e.textContent = value; }
  fetch("{{UPDATE_URL}}", { cache: "no-store" })
    .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : null; })
    .then(function (j) {
      if (!j || !j.versionName) return;
      set("v-name", j.versionName);
      if (j.sizeBytes) set("v-size", (j.sizeBytes / 1048576).toFixed(1));
      if (j.publishedAt) {
        var d = new Date(j.publishedAt);
        if (!isNaN(d)) set("v-date", d.toLocaleDateString(undefined, { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric" }));
      }
      set("v-sha", j.apkSha256);
    })
    .catch(function () {});
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
